WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:02.409
Hello Internet!

00:00:02.409 --> 00:00:04.820
Welcome to Film Theory

00:00:04.820 --> 00:00:08.040
and the third and final episode of our short little foray

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:09.340
into investigative journalism.

00:00:09.340 --> 00:00:10.500
Thanks for sticking with it!

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:12.600
I know that this one was a bit of a departure for the channel,

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:15.760
but honestly this was a personal passion project for me.

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:17.400
After watching Shane's series on Jake,

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:20.320
there were three main topics that I wanted to address.

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:24.440
First, as a creator on this platform for a huge chunk of my life

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:27.840
I wanted to explore the ulterior motives behind this new generation of YouTuber

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:31.700
and the way that they, and the corporations often times funding them,

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:33.240
could be exploiting audience trust.

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:36.060
Second, as a father and an advocate for child education,

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:39.780
I wanted to raise awareness of the manipulation inherent in advertising to kids

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:43.740
and why it's so easy to do; whether it was intentional or not.

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:47.300
and lastly we come to today's episode, where as a viewer

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:51.020
I wanted to take a step back and cover Shane's trend of docu-series

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:55.160
in general, to raise awareness to him and more importantly to you

00:00:55.160 --> 00:01:00.560
about the dangerous tactics that storytellers can use to sway your opinions about the characters and their stories.

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:01.880
Now, as I've done these last few episodes

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:06.560
I've seen a lot of comments from you all watching that reflected a lot of my own feelings

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:08.940
when I was watching Shane's series on Jake for the first time.

00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:11.939
"I kinda wish Shane didn't hold back. He [was] too empathetic."

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:17.759
"I love Shane, but sometimes his videos in this category aren't always revealing the entire truth

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:20.460
or that he's not going as in-depth as he could of when seeking the truth."

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:26.260
"He promoted this thing as nothing is off the table. [But] it was clear that some things were off the table."

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:31.120
I personally wanted to see Jake Paul answer for things like his business decisions and his merch,

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:35.200
but instead it all just felt like one big redemption arc for him.

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:39.280
Which raises the question: Should Shane have covered all that "business stuff"?

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:41.360
Does it matter that he didn't?

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:47.260
And most importantly, are his docu-series actually dangerous because of the way he portrays the people in them?

00:01:47.260 --> 00:01:52.280
Almost as soon as the Jake Paul documentary started airing, Shane came under fire for giving Jake a platform,

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:56.780
and then for cranking up the drama on his story by comparing him to sociopaths.

00:01:56.780 --> 00:02:01.220
And then, by playing dramatic music and using editing tricks to emphasize specific moments,

00:02:01.220 --> 00:02:06.000
and finally for making excuses for Jake's bad behavior by shifting blame to his parents and his brother.

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:10.140
So was the internet right to be leveling criticisms at Shane?

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:11.940
Today, we're gonna find out.

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:15.880
Oh, and, uh, in case you were sick of the guy, this theory isn't about Jake anymore.

00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:20.380
No. This time the whole bank of Shane Daw-sumentaries are under my theoretical microscope.

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:22.140
Shane, "Oh, I'm nervous"

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:23.860
MatPat: You probably should be.

00:02:23.860 --> 00:02:26.940
Or maybe not! The only way to find out is to dive in.

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:32.180
The first - and perhaps, biggest complaint that Shane got in his coverage of Jake Paul was his use of music

00:02:32.180 --> 00:02:35.000
to overemphasize certain key moments early on in the series.

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:37.000
Jake Paul might be a sociopath,

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:39.760
*dramatic chord plays*

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:41.460
he has a creepy dad,

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:44.060
*eerie, high-pitched violin chord*

00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:47.260
It felt to many viewers disingenuous, manipulative.

00:02:47.260 --> 00:02:50.640
And in response, it's something that Shane held back on as the series went on.

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:54.040
But even shots like this, the final one of the series,

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:58.580
*depressing, slow piano with just as depressing, sad vocals plays*

00:02:58.660 --> 00:03:02.800
in slow-mo with somber piano music playing underneath is manipulative,

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:06.160
turning a simple walk away from the camera into something much more:

00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:11.520
a deep, meaningful, reflective moment where Jake's fake persona is finally dropped.

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:16.380
A moment that screams, "This! This right here, is the real Jake Paul!"

00:03:16.660 --> 00:03:19.340
When, seriously, he was just walking away from the camera.

00:03:19.340 --> 00:03:23.920
All of these things: the horror stings, the glitches, the slo-mo shots and sad music

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:28.120
are all examples of the psychological principle known as priming.

00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:32.560
Just like you coat a wall with primer before laying down the paint so that the paint goes on smoother,

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:37.680
You prime the audience so that they more easily accept what you're trying to convince them of.

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:42.020
*gradually more eerily* You know how you recognize that there's about to be a jump-scare in a horror movie

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:47.140
because you're entering a scary place, and then a door starts creaking open and...

00:03:48.260 --> 00:03:49.680
the music changes?

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:50.820
*normally* That's a form of priming.

00:03:50.820 --> 00:03:52.820
But it's not all about just about music and editing,

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:58.340
Everyone has made the joke that it takes a looong time for a Shane docu-series to get started.

